[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":130},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":-5,"_45":46,"_47":48,"_49":50,"_51":52,"_53":36,"_54":55,"_56":57,"_58":36,"_59":60},"expiresAt",1795740038000,"id","j2m3d42a8fp6p1wtfkbf047ok","title","Recorded Future 解析战略威胁情报的定义、来源与需求方式","originalTitle","What is Strategic Threat Intelligence? | Recorded Future","summary","Recorded Future 将战略威胁情报定位为面向高管和董事会的非技术、风险导向型情报，用于评估业务决策及组织威胁态势。其主要来源为 OSINT，因信息量大且常含外语，手工收集难度较高，合适工具可批量检索、实时识别并自动翻译。组织宜提出关于风险趋势与可能影响的问题，而非要求预测具体攻击者、地点和方式。","reason","source",{"_70":129},"links",{"_60":128},"publishedAt","2026-11-26T00:00:00.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-26T16:44:20.808Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[125,126,127],"score",11,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_75":76,"_13":77},"x","readingMode","full","author","body",{"_121":-5,"_60":122,"_123":-5,"_124":36},"outline",[78,79,80,81,82,83,84,85,86,87,88,89,90],"relatedStories",[74],"topics",[67,68],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_61":62,"_63":64,"_65":66},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fKyGQimVmk5s","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_69":72,"_70":73},{"_69":30,"_70":71},"slug","name","蓝队与运营","compliance","合规与标准",{"_75":76,"_13":77},"publicId","d8e7d473-babf-456e-a070-c7b15022d020","Recorded Future发布战略威胁情报指南",{"_11":119,"_92":120,"_94":106},{"_11":117,"_92":118,"_94":106},{"_11":115,"_92":116,"_94":106},{"_11":113,"_92":114,"_94":106},{"_11":111,"_92":112,"_94":106},{"_11":109,"_92":110,"_94":106},{"_11":107,"_92":108,"_94":106},{"_11":104,"_92":105,"_94":106},{"_11":102,"_92":103,"_94":95},{"_11":100,"_92":101,"_94":95},{"_11":98,"_92":99,"_94":95},{"_11":96,"_92":97,"_94":95},{"_11":91,"_92":93,"_94":95},"sec-13","text","How can Recorded Future help produce strategic threat intelligence?","level",3,"sec-12","How long does it typically take to produce a strategic threat intelligence repor","sec-11","What are the benefits of using strategic threat intelligence for business decisi","sec-10","What are some real-world examples of strategic threat intelligence?","sec-9","What is the difference between strategic and operational threat intelligence for","sec-8","Frequently Asked Questions",2,"sec-7","What’s the Difference Between Strategic and Tactical Threat Intelligence?","sec-6","How Is Strategic Threat Intelligence Success Evaluated?","sec-5","How Should Organizations Request Strategic Threat Intelligence?","sec-4","Who Produces Strategic Threat Intelligence?","sec-3","Sources of Strategic Threat Intelligence","sec-2","What Is Strategic Threat Intelligence?","sec-1","Key Takeaways","zh","<h2 id=\"sec-1\">Key Takeaways</h2><ul>\n          <li>Threat intelligence is often thought of as a single function, but in reality, it can be broken down into four categories: strategic, tactical, operational, and technical.</li>\n          <li>Strategic threat intelligence is non-technical and is used by high-level strategists to inform specific decisions.</li>\n          <li>For the most part, strategic threat intelligence comes from freely available sources. However, the volume of available sources, combined with language constraints, often makes manual collection infeasible.</li>\n          <li>The success of strategic threat intelligence depends on strong two-way communication between threat analysts and their primary audience — usually the board of directors.</li>\n        </ul><p>On the surface, <a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/threat-intelligence\">threat intelligence</a> appears to be a single discipline, closely intertwined with the <a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/threat-intelligence-lifecycle-phases\">cyber threat intelligence cycle</a>. Practitioners collect data from a variety of sources, analyze it, and use the outputs to make decisions.</p><p>The reality is a little more complicated. As with most specialties, threat intelligence can be broken down into subcategories, each with its own uses, techniques, and challenges. Here are the three distinct categories that threat intelligence is typically boiled down to:</p><ul>\n          <li><strong>Strategic Intelligence</strong>: Non-technical, risk-based intelligence used by high-level decision makers.</li>\n          <li><strong><a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/tactical-threat-intelligence\">Tactical Intelligence</a></strong>: Details of threat actor tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs).</li>\n          <li><strong><a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/operational-threat-intelligence\">Operational Intelligence</a></strong>: Actionable information about specific incoming attacks.</li>\n        </ul><p>In this blog series, we’ll take an in-depth look at each of these categories, including how they are collected, analyzed, and used to enhance security.</p><p>Today, we’re covering strategic intelligence.</p><h2 id=\"sec-2\">What Is Strategic Threat Intelligence?</h2><p>Strategic threat intelligence is non-technical, risk-focused intelligence that provides executives and board members with a bird’s-eye view of an organization’s threat landscape, enabling them to understand the broader impact of high-level business decisions. It isn’t concerned with specific actors, indicators, or attacks.</p><p>Given that the audience is primarily C-suite and board level, strategic threat intelligence is almost exclusively non-technical. Instead, it covers factors such as risk scores and the possible outcomes of a given action or decision, such as entering a foreign market or taking an ideological position.</p><p>Since it’s used to inform specific, high-level decisions, strategic threat intelligence is usually gathered on demand rather than as an ongoing initiative, and is most often presented as a report or briefing.</p><h2 id=\"sec-3\">Sources of Strategic Threat Intelligence</h2><p>Unlike other intelligence categories, the majority of strategic threat intelligence sources are Open Source Intelligence, or <a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/open-source-intelligence-definition\">OSINT</a>, meaning they can be freely accessed by anyone who cares to do so. Common examples include:</p><ul>\n          <li>Policy documents from nation-states and other groups of interest</li>\n          <li>Local and national media</li>\n          <li>Industry- and subject-specific publications</li>\n          <li>Comments, online activity, and articles from individuals of interest</li>\n          <li>Free content produced by security organizations (e.g., white papers, research reports, etc.)</li>\n        </ul><p>While the accessibility of strategic sources can be a tremendous positive, it can also be a double-edged sword, as analysts must manually process huge quantities of raw data in order to identify valuable insights. Worse still, the most valuable insights are often “hidden” in foreign language sources, forcing analysts to spend even more time on translation.</p><p>Fortunately, if analysts are armed with the right tools, these difficulties can largely be sidestepped. Powerful threat intelligence solutions can scour a large volume of sources, identify relevant information in real time, and automatically translate non-native results.</p><h2 id=\"sec-4\">Who Produces Strategic Threat Intelligence?</h2><p>Perhaps the most significant difference between strategic threat intelligence and other intelligence categories is the skill set needed for production. While typical security and analysis skills are still essential, producing strategic threat intelligence also requires a great deal of expertise in other areas — in particular, a strong understanding of sociopolitical and business concepts.</p><p>Since this broad skill set is rarely found in a single individual, some organizations hire analysts with state or military intelligence backgrounds and train them in the security-specific subject areas required for the role. While this approach takes time and resources to pull off, it is often quicker and more effective than holding out for the perfect applicant.</p><h2 id=\"sec-5\">How Should Organizations Request Strategic Threat Intelligence?</h2><p>Strategic threat intelligence stands apart from the other three categories because it’s almost exclusively requested by (and produced for) a non-technical audience. While the outputs are produced in a format that senior executives and board members will understand, the audience’s lack of understanding of what is and isn’t possible can lead them to make requests that non-government analysts simply can’t meet.</p><p>For example, if an organization’s board were considering expansion into another country, it might call on its threat intelligence analysts to provide some insights. As we’ve already seen, though, strategic threat intelligence is very much a “made-to-order” discipline, meaning analysts will do their best to meet the specific requirements of their board.</p><p>This is where “asking good questions” comes in. An inexperienced (in the context of threat intelligence) board might be tempted to demand, “Tell us how, where, and by whom we’ll be attacked if we open this branch.”</p><p>Requirements like this are highly unlikely to lead to valuable insights for two reasons:</p><ul>\n          <li>Acquiring detailed intelligence on specific local or national actors is often impossible for non-state actors.</li>\n          <li>Attempting to predict specific attacks is far less useful (and reliable) than understanding the most common threat trends and their relative likelihoods.</li>\n        </ul><p>A far better line of questioning would be, “Is this expansion likely to open us up to increased cyber risk? If it is, which threat vectors are most likely to be employed, and what would be the cost of preparing for or responding to those threats?” This type of question gives analysts far greater scope to explore useful avenues of research and is much more likely to result in an insightful and actionable intelligence product.</p><p>Ideally, there should be an open line of communication between an organization’s board and its threat intelligence specialists — most likely via the CISO — to ensure that strategic threat intelligence project parameters are set in a way that’s conducive to producing an actionable output.</p><h2 id=\"sec-6\">How Is Strategic Threat Intelligence Success Evaluated?</h2><p>As we’ve already seen, strategic threat intelligence outputs rarely include binary “yes or no” recommendations, focusing instead on variables such as risk and confidence scores. But that’s not to say that evaluation can’t (or shouldn’t) be attempted.</p><p>A strong feedback loop is essential to the consistent production of high-quality intelligence products. Just like any intelligence initiative, a strategic threat intelligence capability should be subject to ongoing evaluation.</p><p>This evaluation process should include feedback from the intelligence team’s primary audience — typically the board of directors — and answer key questions, such as:</p><ul>\n          <li>Was the intelligence produced in line with requirements?</li>\n          <li>How helpful was the intelligence in making the stated decision?</li>\n          <li>Was the intelligence pitched correctly for its (likely non-technical) audience?</li>\n        </ul><p>This type of feedback will typically be collected informally by the CISO and should be used to improve future strategic intelligence gathering and presentation. Of course, one obvious question remains: Was the intelligence proven accurate over time? Unfortunately, this question often proves extremely hard, if not impossible, to answer, for two primary reasons:</p><ul>\n          <li>Strategic intelligence deals primarily with measurements of risk and likelihood, so even if an anticipated result doesn’t occur, that doesn’t mean the intelligence was inaccurate.</li>\n          <li>It’s rarely possible to determine precisely what has (or hasn’t) happened in a remote situation.</li>\n        </ul><p>With all that said, the accuracy of strategic intelligence should be monitored as far as possible and used to improve processes and intelligence outputs.</p><h2 id=\"sec-7\">What’s the Difference Between Strategic and Tactical Threat Intelligence?</h2><p>As we’ve already seen, threat intelligence breaks down into three primary categories — strategic, tactical, and operational — and it’s easy to conflate strategic with tactical since both inform security decisions. The difference comes down to <em>who’s using the intelligence and what they do with it.</em></p><p><strong>Strategic Threat Intelligence</strong></p><p><strong>Tactical Threat Intelligence</strong></p><p><strong>Audience</strong></p><p>Executives, boards, and other high-level decision-makers</p><p>Security analysts, SOC teams, and incident responders</p><p><strong>Focus</strong></p><p>Broad risk trends and business impact</p><p>Specific threat actor tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs)</p><p><strong>Data Sources</strong></p><p>Open-source policy documents, media, and industry research</p><p>Malware samples, attack patterns, and technical indicators of compromise</p><p><strong>Output Format</strong></p><p>Reports and briefings for non-technical stakeholders</p><p>Technical indicators that feed directly into security tools</p><p><strong>Primary Use</strong></p><p>Informing decisions like market expansion or risk tolerance</p><p>Detecting and defending against specific, active threats</p><p><strong>In short</strong>: strategic intelligence tells leadership <em>whether</em> and <em>why</em> a risk matters to the business, while tactical intelligence tells defenders <em>how</em> an attack works so they can stop it. For a closer look at how tactical intelligence is gathered and applied, see our breakdown of <a href=\"https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/tactical-threat-intelligence\">tactical threat intelligence</a>.</p><h2 id=\"sec-8\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3 id=\"sec-9\">What is the difference between strategic and operational threat intelligence for government agencies?</h3><p>Strategic intelligence informs long-term policy and risk decisions for government leadership, while operational intelligence supports response to specific, imminent threats or incidents — the same distinction that applies in the private sector, though government strategic intelligence often incorporates geopolitical and nation-state sourcing more heavily.</p><h3 id=\"sec-10\">What are some real-world examples of strategic threat intelligence?</h3><p>Examples include geopolitical risk assessments before entering a new market, industry-wide threat landscape reports for board presentations, and executive briefings on how a competitor’s breach might signal shared industry risk.</p><h3 id=\"sec-11\">What are the benefits of using strategic threat intelligence for business decisions?</h3><p>It gives leadership a clearer, evidence-based view of risk before making high-stakes calls — like market expansion, M&amp;A, or vendor selection — reducing the chance of decisions made on incomplete or purely anecdotal information.</p><h3 id=\"sec-12\">How long does it typically take to produce a strategic threat intelligence report?</h3><p>Timelines vary by scope, but since most strategic intelligence is made-to-order rather than continuously monitored, turnaround often ranges from a few days for a narrow question to several weeks for a comprehensive board-level briefing.</p><h3 id=\"sec-13\">How can Recorded Future help produce strategic threat intelligence?</h3><p>Recorded Future’s Cyber Ops solution, powered by the Intelligence Graph®, automatically indexes and translates open-source intelligence at scale, giving analysts the bandwidth to focus on high-level analysis instead of manual data gathering.</p>","zhKind","complete","蓝队","安全运营","合规","https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/strategic-threat-intelligence-explained","Recorded Future",{"_131":132,"_133":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
